{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Teaching in Tech-Rich Classrooms With TeachGen@i","title":"Teacher Noticing and Revision","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting teacher noticing and revision to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"02","section":"06","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat531-supplement-02-06-lo-01","statement":"Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-02-06-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-02-06-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-02-06-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible"}],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i","cat531_supplement_teacher_noticing_and_revision","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/02/05"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_2_6_teacher_noticing_and_revision_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_2_6_teacher_noticing_and_revision_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_2_6_teacher_noticing_and_revision_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_2_6_teacher_noticing_and_revision_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":6,"top_tier_hardened":false,"lo_practice_map":{"cat531-supplement-02-06-lo-01":["cat531_supplement_2_6_teacher_noticing_and_revision_calibration"],"cat531-supplement-02-06-lo-02":["cat531_supplement_2_6_teacher_noticing_and_revision_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_2_6_teacher_noticing_and_revision_artifact_signal"],"cat531-supplement-02-06-lo-03":["cat531_supplement_2_6_teacher_noticing_and_revision_annotation_signal"],"cat531-supplement-02-06-lo-04":["cat531_supplement_2_6_teacher_noticing_and_revision_annotation_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Teacher Noticing and Revision\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Teacher Noticing And Revision, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/02-06-teacher-noticing-and-revision.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Teacher Noticing and Revision: Teacher Noticing And Revision, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nTeacher noticing is the professional skill of attending to what students actually do (in their work, their questions, their errors) and interpreting it before deciding what to change. It has two halves that novices tend to collapse: describing what happened and judging what it means. Strong noticing keeps them separate, because a description can be checked by a colleague while a verdict cannot. \"The class does not get geometry\" is a verdict with no foothold for action; \"six of twenty tickets gave perimeter when the question asked for area\" is a description that points to a specific revision. This section trains the discipline of grounding every instructional change in a described observation and a distinction between a one-off moment and a real pattern.\n\nThe artifact, a Design Tension Studio map, records the chain from observation to interpretation to revision, and the alternative revision you rejected. A reviewer should be able to trace why the change was made, not merely that something changed after a hard day.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA teacher finishes a geometry lesson feeling that it went badly and reaches for the verdict \"they just do not get it.\" That sentence ends inquiry; there is nothing in it to act on, and tomorrow's revision would be a guess. Then she actually reads the twenty exit tickets. Six students gave the perimeter when the question asked for area, and four of those six wrote the correct area formula before adding the side lengths instead of multiplying. The verdict said the class failed at geometry. The description says the class knows the formula and is confusing two concepts. Those point to opposite revisions. Noticing is the difference between reteaching everything and fixing the one confusion the evidence reveals.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nNoticing has a structure: observe, then interpret, then decide (the attend, interpret, and decide-to-respond framework of van Es & Sherin, 2021), and a revision is only defensible when each step is visible and linked to the next. Two distinctions do most of the work.\n\nThe first is description versus verdict. A description reports what was on the page or in the room in terms another teacher could verify (\"six tickets swapped area and perimeter\"). A verdict reports a conclusion that skips the evidence (\"they do not get geometry\"). Verdicts feel like noticing but cannot be audited, and they tend to trigger overbroad revisions.\n\nThe second is moment versus pattern. One confused student is a moment, handled with a quick check-in. The same error across a third of the class is a pattern that justifies changing the next lesson. Conflating them leads to two failures: overreacting to a single off comment with a whole-unit overhaul, or ignoring a class-wide pattern because it surfaced quietly. The defensible revision names the pattern, links it to a described observation, and chooses a change that targets the diagnosed cause rather than a generic \"do it again.\" Teacher professional judgment is exactly this disciplined chain; AI tools can surface aggregate patterns from exit data, but the interpretation of what an error means stays with the teacher who knows the lesson.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Which of these notes is a description a colleague could verify, rather than a verdict?\" explanation=\"A description reports what was on the page in terms another teacher could check. The counts and the specific error are verifiable. The class does not get geometry is a verdict that skips the evidence and triggers an overbroad reteach.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Six of twenty tickets gave perimeter when the question asked for area\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The class just does not get geometry\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"That lesson really flopped today\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nWalk through one annotated judgment call that moves from noticing to revision. A teacher finishes a geometry lesson and reviews twenty exit tickets.\n\n**Step 1. Record the observable, not the verdict.**\n\n> **Weak note (rejected):** \"The class just does not get geometry.\" That is a verdict with nothing to act on.\n\n> **Noticing note (kept):** \"Six of twenty exit tickets gave the perimeter when the question asked for area; four of those six wrote the correct area formula but then added the side lengths instead of multiplying.\" The teacher described what was on the page before deciding what it meant.\n\n**Step 2. Weigh the moment against the pattern.** One confused student would warrant a quick check. Six tickets showing the same area-versus-perimeter swap is a pattern worth a revision, and the teacher notes that distinction so a single reaction does not trigger an overhaul.\n\n**Step 3. Make the cause-linked revision.** Recorded on the map: \"Because six tickets confused area with perimeter, tomorrow opens with a two-minute sort: students place six tasks under 'measures the inside' or 'measures the border' before any formula appears. Rejected option: reteach the area formula again, because the tickets show the formula is known; the two ideas are being confused, not the procedure.\"\n\n**Step 4. Leave the link visible.** The map now ties the described observation directly to the revision and explains the rejected alternative. A mentor can trace why the change was made, not just that a change happened.\n\nPut the noticing chain in the order a defensible revision follows. Skipping or reordering a step is how a reaction gets dressed up as noticing.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Describe the observable: six of twenty tickets swapped area and perimeter, four had the correct formula\", \"Distinguish moment from pattern: six tickets is a class-wide pattern, not a single confused student\", \"Interpret the cause: the formula is known; area and perimeter are being confused\", \"Decide the cause-linked revision: open with a sort by what is measured, not a formula reteach\", \"Record the rejected alternative and its reason: reteaching the formula is ruled out because the formula is already correct\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nDescribe before you judge, distinguish a moment from a pattern, and link every revision to the specific observation that motivated it. A revision that cannot name the observed evidence behind it is a reaction, not a professional decision.\n\nBefore committing a revision, check the chain:\n\n1. **Describe:** Is my note a verifiable observation or an unfalsifiable verdict?\n2. **Scale:** Is this a single moment or a pattern across enough students to justify changing the plan?\n3. **Cause:** Does my revision target the diagnosed cause (a conceptual confusion) rather than a generic redo?\n4. **Reject:** What plausible revision did I rule out, and why does the evidence rule it out?\n\nIf the revision floats free of a described observation, the chain is broken.\n\nWalk the geometry exit-ticket decision and watch each move either ground the revision in evidence or float free of it.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"From a hard day to a defensible revision\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"The geometry lesson felt like it went badly. You have twenty exit tickets in your hand. What do you do first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Write the note: the class just does not get geometry, and plan a full reteach\",\"next\":\"verdict\",\"feedback\":\"That is a verdict with nothing to act on. It triggers an overbroad reteach and skips the evidence on the page.\"},{\"label\":\"Read the tickets and describe what is actually on them\",\"next\":\"describe\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Describe before you judge: a description can be checked by a colleague; a verdict cannot.\"},{\"label\":\"One student looked very confused, so overhaul the whole unit\",\"next\":\"overreact\",\"feedback\":\"That overreacts to a single salient moment while a quiet class-wide gap may go unaddressed.\"}]},\"verdict\":{\"prompt\":\"The reteach repeats the area formula. But you never checked the tickets. What is the risk?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"You may reteach something students already know and miss the real confusion\",\"next\":\"describe\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. Read the tickets and describe the observable before deciding.\"}]},\"overreact\":{\"prompt\":\"Before overhauling, you ask: is this a moment or a pattern? How do you tell?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Count how many tickets show the same error\",\"next\":\"describe\",\"feedback\":\"Scale the evidence: one student is a moment for a check-in; a third of the class is a pattern worth a revision.\"}]},\"describe\":{\"prompt\":\"You find six of twenty tickets gave perimeter for an area question, and four of those wrote the correct area formula before adding the sides. What does the evidence point to?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The formula is known; area and perimeter are being confused, so revise the concept distinction\",\"next\":\"done\",\"feedback\":\"You linked the revision to the diagnosed cause and can defend rejecting a formula reteach.\"},{\"label\":\"Reteach the area formula tomorrow to be safe\",\"next\":\"verdict\",\"feedback\":\"The tickets show the formula is correct. Reteaching it wastes a day and leaves the real confusion untouched.\"}]},\"done\":{\"prompt\":\"Tomorrow opens with a two-minute sort: students place tasks under measures the inside or measures the border before any formula appears. The rejected reteach is logged with its reason.\",\"feedback\":\"Noticing is observe, then interpret, then decide, with every revision traceable to a described observation a colleague could check.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\nThe first misreading is to treat a strong feeling about a lesson as data. The sense that \"that flopped\" is a starting prompt to look, not a finding to act on. The second is to overcorrect from a single salient moment: one student's loud confusion can trigger a needless overhaul while a quiet class-wide gap goes unaddressed. The third is to reach for the most familiar revision (reteach the formula) regardless of what the evidence shows; if students already wrote the formula correctly, reteaching it wastes a day and leaves the actual confusion untouched. The map's \"rejected option\" line exists to catch this reflex.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the observe-interpret-decide chain to a writing class. The teacher's impression is that \"the essays were disorganized.\" That is a verdict. Reading the drafts produces a description: eleven of twenty-four essays state a claim in the first paragraph but never return to it, while the body paragraphs each introduce a new, unconnected point. That is a pattern, and it is specific: the issue is not organization in general but the missing link between thesis and evidence. The cause-linked revision is a five-minute mini-lesson where students draw an arrow from each body paragraph back to the thesis and rewrite any paragraph whose arrow does not connect. The rejected revision is \"review essay structure,\" which is too broad to touch the specific thesis-to-body break the drafts revealed.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: in the left column write the described observation; in the right column write the interpretation and the revision it justifies. For the geometry case, the left column says \"six tickets swapped area and perimeter; four had the right formula\" and the right column says \"concept confusion, not procedure; revise with a sort task, not a reteach.\" A strong reader can spot when a revision in the right column has no anchor in the left, which is the signature of a reaction dressed up as noticing. Your map is strong when every revision traces back to a line you could show a colleague.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong noticing map records the observation as a verifiable description, with counts or specifics, not as a verdict. It states explicitly whether the issue is a moment or a pattern and on what evidence. It links the revision to the diagnosed cause, so a reviewer can see why this change and not a generic one. And it names the revision it rejected with the reason the evidence rules it out. Polish is a tidy reflection paragraph; evidence is the visible chain from what students did to what you will change because of it.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_teacher_noticing_and_revision\", \"cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n**When the noticer is a tool.** If TeachGen@i (or any analytics dashboard) reports an aggregate pattern (\"engagement dropped in period 3\"), the noticing discipline extends to the tool's claim itself. Ask the same two questions you ask of your own impressions: is the AI's \"pattern\" a verifiable description or a vendor verdict, and does the class-level aggregate hide a subgroup the disaggregated tickets would reveal? Algorithmic-fairness research in education shows aggregate metrics routinely mask subgroup differences (Kizilcec & Lee, 2022), so describe-versus-verdict and moment-versus-pattern apply to machine noticing too.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove noticing from exit tickets to live, in-the-moment classroom observation during a discussion. The structure holds but the evidence is fleeting: you cannot reread a comment the way you reread a ticket. The discipline becomes capturing the description fast (a tally of how many students cited the text versus offered opinion without it) before the impression hardens into a verdict, and waiting for a pattern across several exchanges before changing your questioning. If you can adapt observe-interpret-decide to ephemeral spoken evidence without collapsing into \"the discussion was flat,\" you have learned noticing as a habit of grounding judgment in described observation rather than as a paperwork step tied to tickets.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"A teacher reads eleven of twenty-four essays that state a claim in paragraph one but never return to it, while each body paragraph raises a new unconnected point. The teacher writes the verdict the essays were disorganized and plans to review essay structure. In two or three sentences, explain why that revision is too broad and name the cause-linked revision the described pattern actually justifies.\" answer=\"The verdict the essays were disorganized skips the specific evidence and points to a generic fix that touches everything and nothing. The described pattern is precise: the break is the missing link between thesis and body, not organization in general. The cause-linked revision is a mini-lesson where students draw an arrow from each body paragraph back to the thesis and rewrite any paragraph whose arrow does not connect, which targets the diagnosed break.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/02-06-teacher-noticing-and-revision-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"02.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 02.06. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_teacher_noticing_and_revision\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that your map records a verifiable observation (not a verdict), states whether it is a moment or a pattern, links the revision to the diagnosed cause, and names the rejected revision with its reason.\n\n## References\n\n- Mishra, P., & Koehler, M. J. (2006). Technological pedagogical content knowledge: A framework for teacher knowledge ([TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- Selwyn, N. (2016). *Is technology good for education?* Polity Press. (critical edtech studies)\n- Perrotta, C., & Selwyn, N. (2020). Deep learning goes to school: Toward a relational understanding of AI in education. [*Learning, Media and Technology*](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- van Es, E. A., & Sherin, M. G. (2021). Expanding on prior conceptualizations of teacher noticing. *ZDM Mathematics Education, 53*, 17-27. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11858-020-01211-4\n- Barnhart, T., & van Es, E. (2014). Studying teacher noticing: Examining the relationship among pre-service science teachers' ability to attend, analyze and respond to student thinking. *Teaching and Teacher Education, 45*, 83-93. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tate.2014.09.005\n- Mason, J. (2002). *Researching your own practice: The discipline of noticing*. RoutledgeFalmer. https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203471876\n- Ning, Y., Zhang, C., Xu, B., Zhou, Y., & Wijaya, T. T. (2024). Teachers' AI-TPACK: Exploring the relationship between knowledge elements. *Sustainability, 16*(3), 978. https://doi.org/10.3390/su16030978\n- Pangrazio, L., Selwyn, N., & Cumbo, B. (2023). Tracking technology: Exploring student experiences of school datafication. *Cambridge Journal of Education, 53*(3). https://doi.org/10.1080/0305764x.2023.2215194\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_6_teacher_noticing_and_revision_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_teacher_noticing_and_revision","stem":"Which is a noticing record rather than a premature verdict?","options":["\"Jordan is lazy and never tries.\"","\"During independent work Jordan started, erased twice, then set the pencil down and stared at the prompt for three minutes.\"","\"Jordan does not care about this class.\"","\"Jordan is just a weak student.\""],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Noticing describes the observable behavior in the moment. The other options leap to a global judgment that cannot be acted on or checked.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_6_teacher_noticing_and_revision_noticing_is_evaluation","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i","cat531_supplement_teacher_noticing_and_revision","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-02-06-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_6_teacher_noticing_and_revision_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_teacher_noticing_and_revision","stem":"One student looks confused during a new model. What is the most defensible response before redesigning the lesson?","options":["Scrap the model and rewrite the whole lesson tonight.","Check whether the confusion is shared by glancing at a few other students' work or asking a quick question, then decide.","Ignore it, since one student is not enough to matter.","Assume the model is fine and move on."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"One signal warrants a check, not a redesign. The teacher weighs the moment against the pattern before changing the plan.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_6_teacher_noticing_and_revision_one_signal_revision","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_teacher_noticing_and_revision","evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-02-06-lo-03","cat531-supplement-02-06-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_6_teacher_noticing_and_revision_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","stem":"A teacher asks TeachGen@i to revise tomorrow's lesson after noticing that six of twenty exit tickets confused area with perimeter. What makes this an evidence-based use of the tool?","options":["The revision is driven by a named noticed pattern (six exit tickets) the teacher can show, not by a vague sense the lesson flopped.","The AI rewrote the lesson, so it must be better.","The teacher changed the lesson because it felt stale.","The revision was made because one student complained."],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Evidence-based revision links the change to a documented noticing. The exit-ticket pattern is the cause the teacher can point to.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_6_teacher_noticing_and_revision_revision_without_cause","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_teacher_noticing_and_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-02-06-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_6_teacher_noticing_and_revision_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","stem":"Which Design Tension Studio map entry best documents teacher noticing leading to revision, in a form a mentor could audit?","options":["\"Lesson felt off, so I changed it.\"","Observed (6 of 20 exit tickets swapped area and perimeter) linked to the revision (open tomorrow by sorting shapes into area vs. perimeter tasks) and the rejected option (reteach the formula again) with the reason it would not address the confusion of the two ideas.","\"Revised the lesson.\"","\"Students seemed bored.\""],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A defensible map links a described observation to a revision and explains the rejected alternative, so the reasoning is inspectable.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_6_teacher_noticing_and_revision_revision_without_cause","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_teacher_noticing_and_revision","evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-02-06-lo-01"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_6_teacher_noticing_and_revision_noticing_is_evaluation","pattern":"noticing_collapsed_into_judgment","description":"Jumping from a single classroom moment straight to a verdict about a student without describing what was actually observed","trigger":"teacher records \"Jordan is unmotivated\" instead of the specific observable behavior in the moment","feedback":"Separate what you saw from what it means. Describe the observable first; the interpretation and the revision come after.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_6_teacher_noticing_and_revision_one_signal_revision","pattern":"revision_on_single_data_point","description":"Overhauling a lesson based on one student's reaction or one confusing moment, ignoring the rest of the evidence","trigger":"teacher rewrites the whole plan because one student looked lost","feedback":"Weigh the moment against the pattern. One signal warrants a check, not a redesign; look for whether it recurs.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_6_teacher_noticing_and_revision_revision_without_cause","pattern":"change_logged_without_reason","description":"Recording that a lesson was changed without naming the noticed evidence that prompted the change","trigger":"revision note says what changed but not what was noticed that justified it","feedback":"Link every revision to the moment you noticed. A change with no triggering observation is not yet evidence-based.","rail_action":"explain"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"8dbb8d642c8210e3c72a16535e61c41cb9a245396786e9abe2b8aa402579c0b0","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}